.he "od (1)                       8/16/26                       od (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
od
- octal, decimal, hex, ascii dump
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
od [ -format ] [ file ] [ [ + ] offset [ . ] [ b ] [ label ] ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Od displays file, or its standard input, in one or more dump formats
picked by the first argument.  With no format argument, -o is the
default.  Dumping goes on to the end of the file.
.sp
More than one format character may be given, and each is applied to
the same data, one conversion to a line.
.sp
The offset argument is the byte offset into the file where dumping
begins.  It is taken in octal unless a . is appended, which makes it
decimal, or it begins with x or 0x, which makes it hexadecimal.  A
trailing b multiplies it by 512 and a trailing B by 1024, as a block
count.  The addresses printed are in the same radix as the offset, or
octal if there is none.
.sp
If file is not given, the offset must be written with a leading +.
.sp
The label argument is a pseudo-address shown in parentheses after the
file offset; it is meant for core images and is written like an
offset.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-a	Name the bytes as ASCII characters.

.ti -10
-b	Bytes as unsigned octal.

.ti -10
-c	Bytes as ASCII characters; the non-graphic ones appear as C escapes or octal numbers.

.ti -10
-d	Short words as unsigned decimal.

.ti -10
-o	Short words as unsigned octal - the default; -B means the same.

.ti -10
-x	Short words as unsigned hexadecimal; -h means the same.

.ti -10
-i	Short words as signed decimal.

.ti -10
-D	Long words as unsigned decimal.

.ti -10
-O	Long words as unsigned octal.

.ti -10
-H	Long words as unsigned hexadecimal; -X means the same.

.ti -10
-I	Long words as signed decimal; -l and -L mean the same.

.ti -10
-s[n]	Print runs of graphic characters that end in a null, at least n long (3 by default).

.ti -10
-v	Show every line; by default a line like the one before it is shown as *.

.ti -10
-w[n]	Take n bytes to a line (16 by default, 32 with no n).

.ti -10
-p	With -a, underline the bytes of even parity.

.ti -10
-P	With -a, underline the bytes of odd parity.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The e, f and F conversions are gone with the floating point this
machine does not have; od says so and exits rather than dump question
marks that look like data.
.sp
A width asked for with -w is cut off at 512, the size of the buffer
the input is read into.
.sp
A file name cannot begin with +, a hexadecimal offset cannot be a
block count, and only one file can be named.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
